# Freezing Halide Segregation Under Intense Light for Photostable Perovskite/Silicon Tandem Solar Cells

## abstract
Photo‐induced halide segregation in wide‐bandgap (WBG) perovskite leads to poor stability and limits its application in high‐efficiency tandem solar cells. Here, a simple solution strategy to achieve photostable WBG perovskite solar cells (PSCs) with bandgap of ≈1.67 eV by ionic coupling potassium sorbate with defects at the buried perovskite interface is reported. Moreover, the ionic coupled potassium sorbate (ICPS) enables to control the formation of N‐methyl formamidinium ions that can selectively passivate the perovskite defects at grain boundaries. As a result, the photo‐induced halide segregation in the target perovskite films is frozen under intense light. The target single‐junction WBG PSC achieves a record efficiency of 22.00% with an open‐circuit voltage (VOC) of 1.272 V and photostability of less than 2% decay over 2000 h of operation. Perovskite/Silicon tandem solar cells are also fabricated that achieve an efficiency of 30.72% (certified 30.09% @1.087 cm2), which is the highest efficiency reported to date with a tunneling oxide passivating contact (TOPCon) c‐Si substrate. The encapsulated tandem device can maintain 97% of its initial efficiency after 1000 h of operation.

Photo-induced halide segregation in wide-bandgap (WBG) perovskite leads to poor stability and limits its application in high-efficiency tandem solar cells. Here, a simple solution strategy to achieve photostable WBG perovskite solar cells (PSCs) with bandgap of ${\approx}1.67$ eV by ionic coupling potassium sorbate with defects at the buried perovskite interface is reported. Moreover, the ionic coupled potassium sorbate (ICPS) enables to control the formation of N-methyl formamidinium ions that can selectively passivate the perovskite defects at grain boundaries. As a result, the photo-induced halide segregation in the target perovskite films is frozen under intense light. The target single-junction WBG PSC achieves a record efficiency of $22.00\%$ with an open-circuit voltage $(\boldsymbol{v}_{\mathrm{oc}})$ of 1.272 V and photostability of less than $2\%$ decay over $2000\text{\textperthousand}$ of operation. Perovskite/Silicon tandem solar cells are also fabricated that achieve an efficiency of $30.72\%$ (certified $30.09\%$ $\textcircled{\bullet}1.087\ c m^{2}]$ , which is the highest efficiency reported to date with a tunneling oxide passivating contact (TOPCon) c-Si substrate. The encapsulated tandem device can maintain $97\%$ of its initial efficiency after $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{1000}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{h}}$ of operation.
## 1. Introduction
Over the past decades, metal halide perovskites have attracted great attention due to their tunable bandgap $(E_{\mathrm{g}})$ , excellent photovoltaic properties, and processability.[1–6] The power conversion efficiency (PCE) of single-junction perovskite solar cells (PSCs) has rapidly increased from the initial $3.8\%$ to a certified value of $26.1\%$ .[7,8] Tandem solar cells (TSCs), which integrate widebandgap (WBG) PSCs $(E_{\mathrm{g}}{>}1.65\ \mathrm{eV})$ with other photovoltaic technologies such as crystalline silicon,[9–12] copper indium gallium selenide,[13] or narrow bandgap perovskite, have been proposed as a promising approach to further improving the efficiency.[14–17] For perovskite/silicon tandem solar cells, although perovskite with $E_{\mathrm{g}}$ of $1.73\ \mathrm{eV}$ theoretically achieves optimal efficiency, practical constraints such as parasitic absorption reduce its optimal $E_{\mathrm{g}}$ to ${\approx}1.67$ eV.[18,19]
However, it is noteworthy that the mixed halide WBG perovskites (e.g., $\mathrm{APb}(\mathrm{I}_{1\cdot x}\mathrm{Br}_{x})_{3})$ , especially for $x{>}0.2$ suffer from photo-induced halide segregation into narrowbandgap iodine-rich and wide-bandgap bromide-rich domains, leading to large open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ deficit and poor stability of WBG PSCs.[20–22] Defects within perovskite layers and at the interfaces are key factors contributing to halide segregation.[23–25] These defects trap photogenerated carriers and facilitate the migration of halide ions under illumination, imposing significant challenges for achieving high-performance and stable TSCs.[26–28]
Composition and additive engineering can effectively optimize crystallization and reduce the defect density within WBG perovskites, which are promising approaches to suppressing halide segregation.[29–34] For example, alloying chlorine into the perovskite lattice was demonstrated to stabilize mixed halide perovskite films under intense light,[31] while 2D additives accumulated at grain boundaries could significantly extend the photostability of WBG PSCs.[35] Interface engineering also plays a crucial role in reducing interfacial charge recombination and improving device stability. For instance, self-assembled monolayers were reported to provide fast extraction and efficient passivation at the buried interface, which considerably slowed photoinduced segregation.[36,37] However, the long-term stability of efficient WBG PSCs, especially the photostability, is still a huge challenge for the future application of perovskite-silicon TSCs.
Here we report a simplified and efficient approach to enhancing the photostability of WBG PSCs $(\approx1.67\mathrm{eV})$ by simultaneously reducing defects within perovskite layers and at the interfaces. Specifically, we introduced a certain amount of potassium sorbate into the perovskite precursor solution, where the sorbate ions can couple with defects in perovskite films, accumulating at the buried interface to effectively enhance crystallization and reduce defects at interfaces. Moreover, by regulating the concentration of potassium sorbate and the stirring time of the precursor solution, we found that the formation of N-methyl formamidinium $(\mathrm{MFA^{+}})$ ions in the perovskite precursor solution can be controlled. Although previous studies have reported that the formation of ${\mathrm{MFA^{+}}}$ ions may have adverse effects on perovskite films, this work shows that the controlled formation of ${\mathrm{MFA}}^{+}$ ions plays a crucial role in enhancing the performance of perovskite films. The combined effect of ionic coupled potassium sorbate (ICPS) and ${\mathrm{MFA}}^{+}$ ions significantly improved the uniformity of halide in perovskite films under intense light, which enhanced the performance and photostability of WBG PSCs. As a result, the singlejunction device obtained a record PCE of $22.00\%$ with a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.272\mathrm{V},$ , reaching $92\%$ of the Shockley–Queisser (S–Q) limit. Encapsulated devices retained their initial PCE with less than $2\%$ decay after $2000\mathrm{h}$ under continuous AM 1.5G illumination at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in ambient air. Furthermore, we fabricated perovskite/TOPCon silicon tandem solar cells that exhibited a PCE of $30.72\%$ (certified $30.09\%$ with an area of $1.087\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ ), which is the highest efficiency reported to date with a tunneling oxide passivating contact (TOPCon) c-Si substrate.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Characterization of Perovskite Films and Interfaces
We first performed time-of-flight second ion mass spectroscopy (TOF-SIMS) depth profiling to investigate the spatial distribution of potassium sorbate in WBG PSCs with the device architecture depicted in Figure 1a. The chemical composition of perovskite used in our study was $\mathrm{Cs}_{0.05}(\mathrm{FA}_{0.84}\mathrm{MA}_{0.16})_{0.95}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I}_{0.75}\mathrm{Br}_{0.25})_{3}$ . Our results showed that sorbate ions were primarily localized at the buried interface between the perovskite and NiO layer, whereas potassium ions were observed throughout the perovskite film and accumulated at the buried interface (Figure 1b). This suggests that ion pairs of potassium and sorbate ions aggregated at the perovskite/NiO interface. Interestingly, we observed a strong and uniform signal throughout the perovskite film that did not belong to the perovskite composition or potassium sorbate additive. This signal was identified as ${\mathrm{MFA}}^{+}$ , a cation known to be the reaction product of $\mathrm{MA^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{FA^{+}}$ ions in perovskite precursor solution (Figure S1, Supporting Information).[38]
To confirm the generation of ${\mathrm{MFA}}^{+}$ ions in our perovskite precursor solution, we employed proton nuclear magnetic resonance $({}^{1}\mathrm{H\NMR})$ spectroscopy to compare the spectra of precursor solutions without additives, with potassium sorbate additive, and with N-methyl formamidinium iodide (MFAI) additive. The origin NMR spectra of MFAI and sorbic acid are shown in Figures S2 and S3 (Supporting Information). The results revealed the presence of peaks at ${\approx}3.1$ and ${\approx}8.3$ ppm in both spectra of precursor solutions containing potassium sorbate and MFAI additives, indicating the generation of ${\mathrm{MFA}}^{+}$ ions in precursor solution doped with potassium sorbate (Figure 1c,d). Simultaneously, a broad $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR peak at ${\approx}9.0$ ppm was detected in both the control and MFA spectra, corresponding to the resonance from the protons adjacent to nitrogen of the organic cations. Upon the addition of potassium sorbate, this resonance became narrower and exhibited an upfield shift, which signified the ionic coupling between sorbate ions and amino groups in $\mathrm{MA^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{FA^{+}}$ ions. As reported in the literature, the condensation reaction occurred over prolonged time periods and at elevated temperatures, while the increase of the pKa value of the precursor solution could significantly accelerate this reaction.[39,40] Consequently, we observed the presence of ${\mathrm{MFA}}^{+}$ in precursor solutions after treatment due to the high pKa of ICPS $(\approx12.8)$ , confirming the accelerated reaction kinetics in an alkalinity precursor environment.[41] It is noteworthy that excessive consumption of $\mathrm{MA^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{FA^{+}}$ cations can have a detrimental impact on the resulting perovskite film. In contrast, precise regulation of the ICPS process yielded only a minimal amount of $\mathrm{MFA^{+}}$ (Figure S7, Supporting Information). The perovskite film with small amounts of ${\mathrm{MFA}}^{+}$ ions exhibited enhanced crystallization (Figure S8, Supporting Information). We further undertook a comprehensive investigation into the influence of ${\mathrm{MFA}}^{+}$ in perovskite films, which significantly enhanced the photoelectric performance of the film as demonstrated later in this study.
To investigate the individual contributions of ICPS and ${\mathrm{MFA}}^{+}$ incorporation on the properties of perovskite films, we carried out a series of measurements on perovskite films with the addition of ICPS and MFAI. Results of UV–vis absorption spectroscopy and Tauc plot confirmed that the incorporation of ICPS and MFAI had a negligible impact on the bandgap of our 1.67- eV perovskite (Figure S9, Supporting Information). Notably, the ICPS treatment significantly improved the crystallinity and morphology of perovskite films, as evidenced by higher X-ray diffraction (XRD) peak intensity (Figure 1e) and larger grain size (Figure S10, Supporting Information). We attributed this enhancement to the effect of the carboxylate in sorbate ion, as there was no apparent change in the MFAI counterpart.[42,43]
The interaction of $\mathrm{MFA^{+}}$ and ICPS with perovskite was studied by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements on both the top and bottom surfaces of perovskite films. Consistent with TOF-SIMS results, the results revealed an enrichment of K at the bottom surface of the perovskite film, as evidenced by the strong $\mathtt{K}2\mathtt{p}$ peaks. However, the presence of K signals on the upper surface was nearly indiscernible, indicating a K concentration below the XPS detection limit (Figure S11, Supporting Information). Notably, after ICPS treatment, the binding energies corresponding to the Pb 4f and I 3d core levels of the bottom surface exhibited a shift to lower binding energies by over $0.2\mathrm{eV}.$ This result suggests the increased electron density around Pb and I atoms, which is attributed to the interaction between sorbate ions and the perovskite bottom surface (Figure S12, Supporting Information).[34,44–46] As for the top surface, the Pb 4f peaks and I 3d peaks showed a slight shift to lower and higher binding energies, respectively, demonstrating that a small number of $\mathrm{MFA^{+}}$ ions interacted with the perovskite top surface (Figure S13, Supporting Information).
These results were further validated by first-principles calculations based on density functional theory (DFT). To simplify the calculations, we employed $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ as a model system for DFT modeling, as it represents the main component used in our experimentally studied system. Besides, we selected the (001) $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ -terminated perovskite surface as the calculation model, owing to its confirmed stability and possession of the lowest energy configuration.[47] Based on the results of the electrostatic potential (ESP) analysis (Figure S14, Supporting Information) and charge density difference analysis (Figure S15, Supporting Information), the electron-rich carboxylate in sorbate ion interacted strongly with the electron-poor undercoordinated $\mathrm{{Pb}}$ atom on the optimized perovskite surface. The interaction drove the formation of stable Pb-O bonds and significantly enhanced the electrostatic attraction between perovskite and sorbate ions with a binding energy of $-2.13\ \mathrm{eV}.$ In addition, the electron-deficient - ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{^{+}}$ and electron-rich $\mathsf{\Gamma}=\mathsf{N H}$ groups in the ${\mathrm{MFA}}^{+}$ ion interacted with undercoordinated I and $\mathrm{Pb}$ atoms through hydrogen bonds and ionic bonds, respectively, with a strong binding energy of $-2.69\ \mathrm{eV}.$ XPS analysis combined with DFT simulation confirmed a strong interaction between perovskite and ${\mathrm{MFA}}^{+}$ as well as ICPS, which was beneficial for stabilizing both top and bottom surface components in perovskite.
Next, we performed time-dependent photoluminescence (PL) measurements on both control and modified perovskite films to verify the effect of ICPS and MFAI incorporation on the photostability of perovskite films (details in Experimental Section). During continuous illumination, the PL peak of the control film gradually red-shifted and was accompanied by a rapidly increasing peak at around $780~\mathrm{nm}$ , which indicated the formation of an I-rich phase (Figure 2a). The MFAI-treated film exhibited a slightly red-shifted PL peak center, indicating a minor increase in film inhomogeneity (Figure 2b). In contrast, the PL peak center of the ICPS-treated film remained anchored at $740\mathrm{nm}$ even after exposure to $500\mathrm{~s~}$ of intense light, implying a highly improved phase stability (Figure 2c). Additionally, we evaluated the extent of PL redshift and the formation of double peaks by tracing the spectral centroid of PL peaks over time. As shown in Figure 2d, the wavelength corresponding to the centroid of the control film showed a nearly linear increasing trend under $500\mathrm{s}$ of illumination. On the contrary, the counterpart of MFAI-treated film only increased rapidly at the beginning and then gradually leveled off, while the ICPS-treated film maintained a consistently stable centroid. We also calculated the ratio of the center intensities of PL peaks at 780 and $740~\mathrm{nm}$ , which showed a similar trend to the centroid results (Figure S16, Supporting Information). Furthermore, the PL intensity of the control film dropped rapidly over time, whereas that of MFAI-treated film only decreased in the first $200\mathrm{s}$ and ICPS-treated film maintained a much more stable PL intensity (Figure S17, Supporting Information). These results demonstrated the effectiveness of ICPS incorporation in achieving segregation-free perovskite films under illumination.
We also conducted steady-state PL and time-resolved PL (TRPL) measurements to study the impacts of ICPS and MFAI incorporation on charge transport and recombination kinetics. The decay curves were fitted to the bi-exponential equation of $\tau_{\mathrm{average}}=(A_{1}{\tau_{1}}^{2}+A_{2}{\tau_{2}}^{2})/(A_{1}\tau_{1}+A_{2}\tau_{2})$ , where $\tau_{1}$ represents the fast decay lifetime corresponding to trap-assisted nonradiative recombination and $\tau_{2}$ represents the slow decay lifetime relating to radiative recombination (Table S1, Supporting Information).[48] The PL intensity of ICPS-treated perovskite film was nearly 4 times higher than that of the control film, indicating improved crystallinity and effective passivation under the synergy of potassium and sorbate ions (Figure S19, Supporting Information). Besides, the fitted carrier lifetime of perovskite films treated with ICPS, as determined from the TRPL spectra (Figure 2e), increased remarkedly from 0.43 to $1.8\upmu\mathrm{s}$ , which can be attributed to the suppressed non-radiative charge recombination caused by defects. Furthermore, by adding the same quantity of MFAI as that of potassium sorbate into the precursor solution, we found that MFAI treatment alone led to a considerable enhancement in both PL intensity and carrier lifetime of the perovskite film. However, the improvement achieved through MFAI treatment was not as significant as that obtained through ICPS treatment. These findings suggest that the combination of ICPS and ${\mathrm{MFA}}^{+}$ is more effective than ${\mathrm{MFA}}^{+}$ alone in enhancing the optoelectronic properties of perovskite films.
The impact of ICPS and ${\mathrm{MFA}}^{+}$ incorporation on the interface recombination between perovskite films and hole transport layer (HTL) was investigated through photoluminescence quantum yield (PLQY) on both modified and control films (Figure 2f). For neat perovskite films, the PLQY value of ICPS-modified perovskite film was significantly higher than that of control film and slightly higher than that of MFAI, consistent with the PL and TRPL results mentioned above. Upon contact with a NiO layer, the PLQY value of the control film decreased by an order of magnitude, indicating severe non-radiative recombination at the perovskite/NiO interface. However, MFAI-treated perovskite film showed a less pronounced decrease in PLQY due to the reduced defect density, while the ICPS-treated perovskite film exhibited much less reduction in PLQY due to the effectively suppressed non-radiative recombination both in bulk and at the interface. We also obtained similar results on the device performance as shown in Figure S20 (Supporting Information).
To further study the role of potassium sorbate at the interface between perovskite and NiO layers, we fabricated NiO-PS film by depositing potassium sorbate on a NiO substrate. XPS analysis confirmed the presence of potassium sorbate on the NiO surface, as evidenced by the occurrence of K peaks and the shift of the Ni and O peaks in NiO (Figures S21 and S22, Supporting Information). DFT results also demonstrated the strong interaction between sorbate ions and NiO surface (Figure S15, Supporting Information). Ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) measurements revealed a better energy-level alignment between the ICPS-treated perovskite and NiO, which contributes to more efficient charge transportation and reduced $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ deficit (Figure S23, Supporting Information). These findings were further supported by the devices based on NiO-PS films, which exhibited significantly improved $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ due to reduced non-radiative recombination losses at the buried interface (Figure S24, Supporting Information).
To elucidate the mechanism of defect passivation by sorbate and ${\mathrm{MFA}}^{+}$ ions, we performed DFT calculations to investigate the interaction between these ions and two types of defects: $\mathrm{Pb}$ - I antisite substitution $(\mathrm{Pb}_{\mathrm{I}})$ , a typical deep-level defect on WBG perovskite surface,[49,50] and the common oxygen vacancy $(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{O}})$ defects on the NiO surface.[51] As shown in Figures S25 and S26 (Supporting Information), The interaction between ${\mathrm{MFA}}^{+}$ and sorbate ions with $\mathrm{Pb}_{\mathrm{I}}$ defect, resulted in the effective elimination of deep in-gap states, as evidenced by the calculated density of states (DOS). In addition, the sorbate ion filled the oxygen vacancy on the NiO surface with its oxygen atom, thereby moving the ${\tt V}_{\mathrm{O}}$ defect state away from the in-gap region (Figure S27, Supporting Information).
## 2.2. Single-Junction WBG Perovskite Solar Cells
We fabricated inverted single-junction opaque PSCs with the stack of Glass/ITO/NiO/Perovskite $\mathrm{/C_{60}/B C P/A g}$ , where ITO and BCP denote indium tin oxide and bathocuproine, respectively. Figure 3a shows the current density–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves of the control and ICPS-treated devices under AM1.5G simulated solar illumination. We optimized the concentration of ICPS as well as the stirring time of precursor and found that $2\mathrm{mol}\%$ with stirring $10\mathrm{{h}}$ resulted in the best performance. (Figures S28 and S29, Supporting Information). The champion control device showed a PCE of $19.71\%$ , with a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.18~\mathrm{V},$ a short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{SC}})$ of $20.46~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}$ , and a fill factor (FF) of $81.60\%$ . Compared to the control device, the state-of-the-art ICPS-treated device exhibited a maximum PCE of $22.00\%$ , with an impressively high $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.272\mathrm{V},$ a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $21.09\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , and an FF of $82.01\%$ . The achieved $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.272\mathrm{V},$ reaching $92\%$ of the S-Q limit, can be attributed to the suppression of non-radiative recombination at both the bulk and surface of the WBG perovskite. To the best of our knowledge, this is one of the highest reported $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and PCE values for PSCs with a bandgap suitable for perovskitesilicon tandem cells (Figure 3b). The integrated $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ calculated from the external quantum efficiency (EQE) curves of the control and ICPS-treated devices were 20.73 and $21.27\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , respectively, which were in good agreement with the $J{-}V$ measurements (Figure S30, Supporting Information). From the derivative of EQE curves, we confirmed that the bandgap of the perovskite layer is $1.67\mathrm{eV},$ which is consistent with the UV–vis result mentioned earlier.
The statistical analysis of the PCE results for 20 individual devices in each group confirmed the high reproducibility of the performance improvement achieved by the ICPS incorporation (Figure 3c). The Mott–Schottky plots revealed that the ICPS-treated devices exhibited a built-in potential $(V_{\mathrm{bi}})$ of $1.20~\mathrm{V}_{:}$ , much higher than the control device of $1.09\mathrm{~V},$ which was consistent with the improvement in $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ (Figure S31, Supporting Information).[52] In addition, a comparison of the dark $J{-}V$ curves showed that the ICPS-treated devices had a much lower saturation current, indicating reduced leakage current and non-radiative recombination.[15] Moreover, we performed lightintensity-dependent $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ measurements to further demonstrate the improvement in carrier recombination upon the ICPS incorporation. As shown in Figure 3d, a linear relationship was observed between $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and $l n(I)$ , which conformed to the formula: $V_{\mathrm{OC}}=n k_{\mathrm{B}}T\ln(I)/q+c$ onstant,[53] where $k_{\mathrm{B}}$ is the Boltzmann constant, $T$ is temperature, $\boldsymbol{q}$ is the electric charge, and $n$ is the ideality factor. The ideality factor of the control and ICPS-treated devices were 1.556 and 1.252, respectively. The lower ideality factor indicates that trap-assisted recombination was suppressed in the ICPS-treated devices.[54]
We also fabricated $1.02\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ opaque solar cells. The champion device showed a certified efficiency of $20.68\%$ with negligible hysteresis, which appears to be the highest certified PCE reported for $\scriptstyle1\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ PSCs used in perovskite/silicon tandems (Figure 3e; Figure S32, Supporting Information). The $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of the $1.02\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ device reached $1.273\mathrm{V},$ consistent with that of $0.08~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ device, demonstrating the effectiveness of the ICPS treatment strategy. The $1.02\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ device was measured at the stabilized power output (SPO) for $500\mathrm{s}$ to obtain a stabilized PCE of $20.46\%$ (Figure 3f).
To assess the impact of ICPS incorporation on device stability, we examined the operational stability of encapsulated devices at room-temperature under continuous AM 1.5G illumination in ambient air (ISOS-L-1) (Figure 3g). The ICPS-treated devices retained ${\approx}99\%$ of their initial PCE after $2000\mathrm{~h~}$ , while the control device decreased to ${\approx}80\%$ of its initial PCE after only $718\mathrm{h}$ . We also tracked the thermal operational stability of encapsulated devices by heating at $65~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under continuous AM 1.5G illumination (ISOS-L-2) (Figure S33, Supporting Information). The ICPS-treated devices maintained $80\%$ of their initial efficiency after $602\mathrm{h}$ , whereas the control device had a much shorter lifetime $({\approx}110\mathrm{h})$ . These results demonstrated a remarkable improvement in the photostability of WBG PSCs after ICPS incorporation, which is consistent with the suppressed photo-induced phase segregation and reduced defects in perovskite films.
## 2.3. Monolithic Perovskite/Silicon TSCs
To construct monolithic perovskite/silicon TSCs, we first developed WBG semi-transparent devices with an area of $\approx1.1\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ (Figure S35, Supporting Information). The metal electrode of opaque devices was replaced with an Indium-Zinc-Oxide (IZO) transparent electrode. The $20\ \mathrm{nm\thinspaceSnO_{2}}$ layer was deposited using atomic-layer deposition (ALD) to prevent damage to the electron transport layer during sputtering. We evaporated a designed metal grid and Magnesium fluoride $(\mathrm{MgF}_{x})$ to reduce the series resistance and reflection loss of the top transparent electrode. The top-illuminated semi-transparent device exhibited a PCE of $19.35\%$ , with a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.265\mathrm{V},$ a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $19.651\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , and an FF of $77.84\%$ . The stabilized efficiency was measured to be $19.40\%$ at SPO for $400\mathrm{~s~}$ . However, the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of semi-transparent devices was lower than opaque devices due to the parasitic absorption by front IZO and $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ layers as well as the lack of reflection from the metal electrode, as indicated by the EQE spectra.
We then constructed monolithic perovskite/silicon TSCs by fabricating $1.67~\mathrm{eV}$ ICPS-treated PSCs on silicon bottom cells with tunneling oxide passivating contact (TOPCon) structure. The schematic of the full tandem architecture is illustrated in Figure 4a, while Figure 4b depicts the cross-section SEM image of a section of the tandem device. We deposited $20\mathrm{nm}$ ITO with a designed mask on the n-poly: Si layer to create a recombination layer. Subsequently, $20\mathrm{nm}$ NiO was deposited via magnetron sputtering to serve as the hole transport layer, enhancing the wettability of poly-Si substrate and facilitating complete surface coverage (Figure S36, Supporting Information).
Figure $4c$ shows the $J{-}V$ curves of the most efficient tandem device with a designed aperture area of $1.087\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . The champion tandem device exhibited a PCE of $30.80\%$ with a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.929\mathrm{V},$ a $J_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}}$ of $19.58\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , and an FF of $81.54\%$ with low hysteresis. With the bottom cell exhibiting the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $\approx673~\mathrm{mV}$ under half illumination, the contribution of the perovskite sub-cell exceeded $1.25\mathrm{~V~}$ (Figure S37, Supporting Information). Figure 4d depicts the EQE spectra of the two sub-cells of the tandem device. The integrated $J_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}}$ values were $19.29\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ for the top perovskite cell and $19.40\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ for the bottom silicon cell, consistent with the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ value from $J{-}V$ measurements. The stabilized PCE of $30.72\%$ was obtained at the SPO for 300 s (Figure 4e). The best-performing tandem devices were measured by an independently accredited testing center (SIMIT, Shanghai, China) for certification, where a PCE of $30.09\%$ $\mathrm{\DeltaV_{OC}=1.949\Delta V,}$ $J_{\mathrm{SC}}=19.351\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and $\mathrm{FF}=79.79\%$ ) was validated (Figure
S38, Supporting Information). This certified PCE and $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ value are the highest among the TSCs with the TOPCon bottom cells (Table S3, Supporting Information).
We further examined the long-term operational stability of encapsulated tandem devices at room-temperature under continuous $\mathrm{AM}\ 1.5\mathrm{G}$ illumination in ambient air (ISOS-L-1). As illustrated in Figure $\textstyle4\mathrm{f},$ the tandem device maintained ${\approx}97\%$ of its initial performance after $1000\mathrm{h}$ , demonstrating excellent photostability by ICPS additive treatment.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we developed a simple solution approach to improving the photostability of WBG PSCs by introducing a certain amount of potassium sorbate into the perovskite precursor solution. The combined effects of the ICPS and the generated ${\mathrm{MFA}}^{+}$ ions effectively minimized defects both in perovskite bulk and at the perovskite/NiO interface, significantly suppressing halide segregation under intense light in WBG PSCs. As a result, the single-junction WBG PSC exhibited a record PCE of $22.00\%$ with a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.272\mathrm{~V}.$ Furthermore, the monolithic perovskite/TOPCon silicon tandem devices with ICPS additive treatment exhibited a PCE of $30.72\%$ (certified $30.09\%$ with an area of $1.087\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ ), which maintained $97\%$ of its initial PCE after $1000\mathrm{h}$ of operation. The high $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ together with the promising photostability achieved here indicates an important step toward the application of monolithic perovskite/silicon TSCs, while further extensive research and exploration are imperative for large-scale fabrication and commercial applications.
## 4. Experimental Section
Materials: Lead iodide $(\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ , $99.99\%$ trace metals basis), Formamidine Hydroiodide (FAI, $99.99\%$ , trace metals basis), Methylamine Hydrobromide (MABr, Low water content) were purchased from Tokyo Chemical Industry (TCI). Lead bromide $(\mathsf{P b}\mathsf{B r}_{2}$ , $99.999\%$ trace metals basis), cesium iodide $(\mathsf{C s l},\mathsf{99.9999})$ , and Potassium sorbate $(\geq99.0\%)$ were purchased from Sigma Aldrich. C60, poly (triaryl amine) (PTAA) Bathocuproine (BCP, purity of $99.9\%$ ) were purchased from Xi’an Polymer Light Technology Corporation (China). The synthesis process of MFAI refers to the literature of ref.[38] All the anhydrous solvents were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich.
Perovskite Precursor Solution: For the 1.4 m $\mathsf{C s}_{0.05}(\mathsf{F A}_{0.84}\mathsf{M A}_{0.16})_{0.95}\mathsf{P b}(\mathsf{I}_{0.75}\mathsf{B r}_{0.25})_{3}$ $2m o l\%$ ICPS wide-bandgap perovskite precursor solution, potassium sorbate $(4.2~\mathsf{m g})$ , MABr $(23.8~\mathsf{m g})$ , CsI $(78.2~\mathsf{m g})$ , FAI $(792.1~\mathrm{mg})$ , $\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}$ ( $753.6~\mathsf{m g})$ , and $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ $(452.4~\mathsf{m g})$ were dissolved in $1m L$ mixed solvent of DMF/DMSO $(\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v},$ 4:1). The precursor solution was stirred at $25^{\circ}C$ for $\mathsf{\Delta}_{\mathsf{l}0\mathsf{h}}$ (from dissolving the precursor to depositing the perovskite film) and then filtered through a $0.22\upmu\mathrm{m}$ PTFE membrane.
For the $\ Y.4~\mathsf{M}~\mathsf{C s}_{0.05}(\mathsf{F A}_{0.84}\mathsf{M A}_{0.16})_{0.95}\mathsf{P b}(\mathsf{I}_{0.75}\mathsf{B r}_{0.25})_{3}$ MFA additive wide-bandgap perovskite precursor solution, MFAI $(2.6~\mathsf{m g})$ , MABr $(23.8~\mathsf{m g})$ , CsI $(78.2~\mathsf{m g})$ , FAI $(792.1~\mathrm{mg})$ ), $\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}$ $\left.753.6\ m g\right)$ , and $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ $(452.4~\mathsf{m g})$ were dissolved in $7m L$ mixed solvent of DMF/DMSO $(\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v},$ 4:1). The precursor solution was stirred $\approx\mathsf{l0~h}$ and then filtered through a $0.22\upmu\mathrm{m}$ PTFE membrane.
For the $1.4\bowtie(\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3})_{0.92}(\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3})_{0.08}$ $\boldsymbol{1.58\ \mathrm{eV}}$ perovskite precursor solution, potassium sorbate $(4.2\ m g)$ , MABr $(72.5~\mathsf{m g})$ , FAI $(227.5~\mathsf{m g})$ , $\mathsf{P b}\mathsf{B r}_{2}$ $(47.7~m\ g)$ , and $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ $(671.6\ m g)$ were dissolved in 1 mL mixed solvent of DMF/DMSO $(\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v},4;\mathsf{l})$ . The precursor solution was stirred $\approx\boldsymbol{\mathrm{10~h}}$ and then filtered through a $0.22\upmu\mathrm{m}$ PTFE membrane.
Single Junction Device Fabrication: Indium tin oxide (ITO)-coated substrates $(72\Omega\mathsf{s q-}^{1})$ were etched with zinc powder and diluted hydrochloric acid (HCl) to produce desired patterns. The patterned substrates were sequentially cleaned with soap, deionized water, ethanol, and acetone under ultrasonication, and then dried with dry nitrogen. The Nickel oxide (NiO) hole transport layer was deposited by RF magnetron sputtering at room temperature using NiO target (3 inches), at 90 W and $0.4\mathsf{P a}$ in pure Argon for 15 min. NiO films were then transferred to the ${\sf N}_{2}$ glove box. The glove box temperature was controlled between 18 and $23^{\circ}\mathsf C$ by the integrated airconditioner, and the water and oxygen levels were kept below 0.1 ppm. The wide bandgap perovskite films were fabricated by the anti-solvent method. The wide bandgap perovskite precursor solution was spin-coated onto the glass/ITO/NiO substrates at 1000 rpm. for $\boldsymbol{{\mathsf{10~s}}}$ and 4000 rpm. for $30~\mathsf{s}$ . Ethyl acetate $(750\upmu\up L)$ was dripped on the center of the spinning substrates at $5s$ before the end of spin-coating and the as-prepared perovskite films were subsequently annealed on a hotplate at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $10\mathsf{m i n}$ .
Finally, 1 nm LiF, $20\mathsf{n m}\mathsf{C}_{60}$ , and ${\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{n m}}{\mathsf{B C P}}$ at the rate of $0.2\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ and $80~\mathsf{n m}$ silver electrode at the rate of $\mathsf{1.0\AA s^{-1}}$ were thermally evaporated under a vacuum $(<70^{-5}~\mathsf{P a})$ , respectively.
As for the NiO-PS film, potassium sorbate powers were dissolved in anhydrous IPA with a concentration of $0.7\dot{\mathrm{~\mg~}}\mathsf{m L}^{-1}$ , which was stirred for $\rceil0\mathrm{~h~}$ and then filtered through a $0.22~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ PTFE membrane. The IPA solution with potassium sorbate was spin-coated onto the glass/ITO/NiO substrates at 5000 rpm for $30\mathrm{~s~}$ The films were annealed on a hotplate at ${100~^{\circ}C}$ for 10 min. Subsequently, the substrates were washed dynamically (4000 rpm, 30 s) by dripping $400~\upmu\upiota$ of IPA.
Si/Perovskite Tandem Device Fabrication: The Tunnel Oxide Passivated Contact solar cell (TOPcon) was fabricated as follows: The wafers were $\mathsf{C}z$ phosphorus-doped n-type mono-crystalline silicon. The thickness of the wafers was $\approx170~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . The wafer surface was first textured by traditional industrial alkaline texturing using KOH and an organic additive solution. The boron emitter was formed in a boron diffusion furnace using a ${\mathsf{B B r}}_{3}$ source. This was followed by an industrial $H F/H N O_{3}$ etching process to remove the rear diffused layers. Subsequently, a thin layer of thermal oxide was grown on the rear side and followed by intrinsic poly-Si deposition by low-pressure chemical vapor deposition (LPCVD) at $550^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The intrinsic poly-Si was doped in a ${\mathsf{P O C l}}_{3}$ diffusion furnace to form ${\mathfrak{n}}^{+}$ poly-Si. A single-side KOH etch process was applied to remove the front side wraparound poly-Si. The front side borosilicate glass (BSG) and rear phosphosilicate glass (PSG) layers were removed in HF and cleaned in RCA cleaning and DI water solution. The textured surface was passivated with atomic layer deposition (ALD) ${\mathsf{A l}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{3}$ and capped with PECVD $\mathsf{S i N}_{\times}$ . $\mathsf{A g}/\mathsf{A l}$ paste was screen-printed on the textured surface and fired in a belt furnace to form ohmic contacts. The $\mathsf{S i O}_{\mathsf{x}}$ capping layer was removed by single-side HF fuming. Then $20\ \mathsf{n m}$ ITO recombination layer was deposited by RF magnetron sputtering at room temperature using ITO target (3 inches), at 90 W and $0.4~\mathsf{P a}$ in pure Argon. The wide bandgap perovskite top cell was then deposited onto the ITO intermediate recombination layer same process as the single junction device except that the BCP layer was replaced by $20\mathsf{n m A L D{-}S n O_{2}}$ (Picosun). Then $100\ \mathsf{n m}$ Indium Zinc Oxide (IZO) was deposited by RF magnetron sputtering at room temperature using IZO target (3 inch), at $63\times$ and $0.75\mathsf{P a}$ in pure Argon for $25\mathrm{\min}$ , and the square resistance of IZO film was ${\approx}42\Omega\ \mathsf{s q}^{-1}$ measured by four-point probe. $200\mathsf{n m}$ silver electrode at the rate of $2.0\dot{\mathsf{A}}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ were thermally evaporated through a designed mask with an area of $\mathsf{1.096c m^{2}}$ . Finally, $100\mathsf{n m}$ Magnesium fluoride $(\mathsf{M g F}_{x})$ as an antireflective layer was thermally evaporated at the rate of $1.0{-}2.0\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ .
Perovskite Film Characterizations: Time-of-flight secondary ion mass spectrometry (TOF-SIMS) measurement was recorded by the focused ion beam TOF-SIMS spectrometer (TOF-SIMS 5–100). A ${\mathsf{B i}_{3}}^{+}$ ion beam was used as the ion beam to peel the samples. Liquid $\mathsf{\Pi}^{\mathsf{I}}\mathsf{H}$ nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectra were collected on AVANCE NEO 700 MHz spectrometer. X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of perovskite films were recorded by Rigaku Dmax 2600 diffractometer using $\mathsf{C u}\mathsf{K}\alpha$ radiation with a Nickel filter. The diffraction data were collected with an angular range of $70^{\circ}<2\theta<50^{\circ}$ and $\textbf{\em{\mathscr{\textbf{\em{\alpha}}}}}\theta=0.02^{\circ}$ over $20\mathrm{\min}$ . absorption spectra of perovskite films were recorded by a Shimadzu UV 2450 spectrometry. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images of perovskite films were acquired using Field emission SEM (JSM-7800F). The cross-sectional SEM image of tandem device was observed by Field emission SEM (TESCAN MAIA3 GMU). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was performed on the AXIS Ultra DLD (Kratos) spectrometer. Ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) was measured by Thermo ESCALAB 250 with a non-monochromated He I𝛼 photon source $(\mathsf{h}\nu=27.22\mathsf{e V}_{.}$ . Steadystate photoluminescence (PL) spectra and time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) spectra were obtained by FLS1000. The excitation wavelength was set at $520~\mathsf{n m}$ . The photoluminescence quantum yield (PLQY) was measured by mounting perovskite films in an integrating sphere. Time-dependent PL spectra were obtained by RISE-MAGNA. The excitation was provided by a $532\cdot\mathsf{n m}$ pulsed laser $({\approx}0.01\ \mathrm{mw};\approx]\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ spot diameter; $30\mathrm{-k}\mathsf{H}z$ repetition rate).
Computational Methods: The optimization of the ground-state geometry for the ${\mathsf{M F A}}^{+}$ and sorbate ions was performed with B3LYP functional and $6{-}3\log({\mathsf{d}},{\mathsf{p}})$ basis set using the Gaussian16 package.[55] The analysis of electrostatic potential (ESP) was performed within the Multiwfn program.[56,57]
First-principles calculations based on DFT were carried out using the Vienna Ab initio Simulation Package.[58] In addition, the DFT-D3 method was used for the van der Waals correction and also for dipole corrections for the slab calculations.[59] A plane-wave cut-off energy of 400 eV was used. The energy and force convergence criteria were set to $10^{-5}$ eV and $0.02\ \mathrm{eV}\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-1}$ , respectively. To simulate the surfaces of perovskite and NiO, the $2\times2\times4$ and $2\times2\times3$ supercells were constructed in the slab structures with a vacuum layer thickness of $20\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ . The Uniform Brillouin zone grids of a $2\times2\times1$ k-mesh for all slabs and interfaces were used. For NiO, the mean-field $\mathsf{D F T}{+}U$ (with $U=5.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ ) was used to reasonably treat the strong electron correlation effect associated with Ni 3d state.[60] The binding energies $(\mathsf{E}_{\mathsf{b}})$ of different ions with the perovskite and NiO surfaces were calculated with the equation of $\mathsf{E}_{\mathsf{b}}=$ E( $\mathsf{o t a l})\mathrm{~-~}\mathsf{E}(\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}/\mathsf{N i O})\mathrm{~-~}\mathsf{E}(\mathsf{i o n})$ , where E(total) is the total energy, $\mathsf{E}(\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}/\mathsf{N i O})$ is the energy of $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ or NiO slabs, and E(ion) is the energy of different ions.
Characterization of Solar Cells: Current–Voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves of the perovskite and TSCs were measured with a digital source meter (Keithley 2400) under AM 1.5G illumination ( $[700\ m\mathrm{\bar{W}}\ c m^{-2},$ ) from a dual light source simulator (Wacom Super Solar Simulator, WXS-90S-L2). The light source was calibrated by a standard silicon reference cell (Newport Oriel 91150-KG5). For single-junction perovskite devices, the measurement was conducted forward (from $-0.1$ to 1.3 V) scan and reverse (from 1.3 to $-0.1\vee)$ scan, with the delay time of $50~\mathsf{m}\mathsf{s}$ . For tandem devices, the measurement was conducted forward (from $-0.1$ to 2.0 V) scan and reverse (from 2.0 to $-0.1$ V) scan, with the delay time of $50~\mathsf{m}\mathsf{s}$ . EQE spectra were recorded by CEP-25ML (Bunko Keiki). For single-junction perovskite devices, the spectra were collected under DC mode with a metallic designated shading mask. For tandem devices, the perovskite top cell was measured with an $850~\mathsf{n m}$ near-infrared continuous biased light and a $0.5{\mathrm{~V~}}$ bias voltages. The Si bottom cell was measured with a $460\ \mathsf{n m}$ blue continuous biased light and a $\ensuremath{\mathsf{1.1}}\ensuremath{\mathsf{V}}$ biased voltage. Dark current curves and Mott–Schottky curves were collected on an electrochemical analysis instrument (Zahner, Germany). For dark current measurement, the voltage scanned from $-0.2$ to $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{1.3~V}}$ with the speed of $20~\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ in the dark box. For Mott–Schottky measurement the voltage scanned from $-0.2$ to $\mathsf{l}.4\mathsf{V}$ with the speed of $2\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ in the dark box.
Stability Measurements: The operational stability tests were carried out at the maximum power point (MPP) for the encapsulated devices with continuous AM 1.5G illumination (calibrated by Newport Oriel 91150- KG5), and the temperature of light-soaking chamber was set at 25 and $65^{\circ}C$ . The voltage at the MPP was calculated and applied automatically; the current output of the device was tracked. All devices were encapsulated between two glass sheets $(55~\mathsf{m m}\times55~\mathsf{m m}\times1.5~\mathsf{m m})$ with butyl rubber as an edge sealant in a vacuum laminator by hot press. The upper and lower substrate temperature was set at $90~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . The background pressure was brought to below $\mathsf{l o h P a}$ with 120s, and then the sample was pressed for 300s.
